Lt. Pavel Chekov, played by Walter Koenig.

12
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:46,000
Star Trek: The Motion Picture is a 1979 American science
fiction film directed by Robert Wise.

13
00:47:18,333 --> 00:47:24,333
The Motion Picture is based on and stars the cast of the 1966–1969 television
series Star Trek created by Gene Roddenberry, who serves as producer.

14
00:50:56,666 --> 00:51:02,666
In the film, set in the 2270s, a mysterious and powerful alien cloud known as
V'Ger approaches Earth, destroying everything in its path.

15
00:54:35,000 --> 00:54:41,000
Admiral James T.

16
00:58:13,333 --> 00:58:19,333
Kirk (William Shatner) assumes command of the recently refitted Starship Enterprise to
lead it on a mission to determine V'Ger's origins and save the planet.

17
01:01:51,666 --> 01:01:57,666
When Star Trek was cancelled in 1969, Roddenberry lobbied Paramount
Pictures to continue the franchise through a feature film.

18
01:05:30,000 --> 01:05:36,000
The success of the series in syndication convinced the
studio to begin work on the film in 1975.

19
01:09:08,333 --> 01:09:14,333
A series of writers and scripts did not satisfy
Paramount, and they scrapped the film project.

20
01:12:46,666 --> 01:12:52,666
Instead, Paramount planned on returning the franchise to its roots,
with a new television series titled Star Trek: Phase II.

21
01:16:25,000 --> 01:16:31,000
The box office success of Star Wars and Close Encounters of the Third Kind convinced Paramount
to change course, cancelling production of Phase II and resuming work on a film.

22
01:20:03,333 --> 01:20:09,333
In March 1978, Paramount announced Wise would direct a $15 million
film adaptation of the original television series.

23
01:23:41,666 --> 01:23:47,666
Filming began that August and concluded the following January.

24
01:27:20,000 --> 01:27:26,000
With the cancellation of Phase II, writers rushed to adapt its
planned pilot episode, "In Thy Image", into a film script.

25
01:30:58,333 --> 01:31:04,333
Constant revisions to the story and the shooting script continued to
the extent of hourly script updates on shooting dates.

26
01:34:36,666 --> 01:34:42,666
The Enterprise was modified inside and out, costume designer Robert Fletcher provided new
uniforms, and production designer Harold Michelson fabricated new sets.

27
01:38:15,000 --> 01:38:21,000
Jerry Goldsmith composed the film's score, beginning an
association with Star Trek that would continue until 2002.

28
01:41:53,333 --> 01:41:59,333
When the original contractors for the optical effects proved unable to complete their tasks in time,
effects supervisor Douglas Trumbull was asked to meet the film's December 1979 release date.

29
01:45:31,666 --> 01:45:37,666
Wise took the just-completed film to its Washington, D.C., opening, but always felt that
the final theatrical version was a rough cut of the film he wanted to make.

30
01:49:10,000 --> 01:49:16,000
Released in North America on December 7, 1979, Star Trek: The Motion Picture received mixed reviews,
many of which faulted it for a lack of action scenes and over-reliance on special effects.

31
01:52:48,333 --> 01:52:54,333
Its final production cost ballooned to approximately $44 million, and it earned $139 million worldwide,
short of studio expectations but enough for Paramount to propose a less expensive sequel.

32
01:56:26,666 --> 01:56:32,666
Roddenberry was forced out of creative control for the
sequel, Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan (1982).

33
02:00:05,000 --> 02:00:11,000
In 2001, Wise oversaw a director's cut for a special DVD release of the film, with
remastered audio, tightened and added scenes, and new computer-generated effects.

34
02:03:43,333 --> 02:03:49,333
The film explores themes of spacecraft, man vs machine, artificial
intelligence (a.i.), self sacrifice, teleportation.

35
02:07:21,666 --> 02:07:27,666
The tone is lighthearted and thought_provoking and mind_bending.
